---
title: Remote control for pi coding agent
description: Run pi on your own machine and control its sessions from a phone or browser with Pontia, while keeping the native pi TUI.
published: "2026-11-11"
---

[pi](https://pi.dev) is a terminal coding agent. Its sessions live in a terminal, which makes it hard to check on a long task from your phone or continue it from another computer. Pontia adds a browser Dashboard to pi without replacing the TUI: both work on the same conversation.

## What you can do

From the Remote Dashboard at [app.pontia.dev](https://app.pontia.dev) you can:

- start a pi session in a project directory on your machine;
- follow the reply and tool output as they stream;
- send messages, or queue them while pi is working;
- interrupt the current turn;
- edit an earlier message to branch the conversation from that point;
- change the model with `/model` and compact the context with `/compact`;
- see estimated context usage;
- reference files with `@`.

pi keeps running on your machine with its own provider credentials and configuration.

## How it works

Pontia's pi plugin connects pi to `pontiad`, the Pontia service on your machine. When you start a session from the Dashboard, Pontia launches pi inside a tmux pane in the selected project. The plugin reports pi's conversation to Pontia and accepts controls from the Dashboard. Conversation history stays in pi's own session files.

Because pi runs in a real tmux pane, you can attach to it from a terminal and use the TUI at any time. Messages you type in the TUI and messages you send from the Dashboard go to the same conversation.

The browser reaches your machine through an Edge relay over an end-to-end encrypted session. The relay forwards ciphertext and cannot read your prompts or files.

## Set it up

You need Linux or macOS 11 or later, with `pi` and `tmux` installed for your user:

```sh
pi --version
tmux -V
```

Configure your model provider in pi first. If you have not, open `pi` and run `/login` and `/model`.

Install Pontia and run the initializer:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://get.pontia.dev/install.sh | sh
pontia init
```

pi is selected by default. The initializer installs the plugin with:

```sh
pi install npm:@pontia/pi-client-plugin
```

It then starts the Pontia service, signs you in to Pontia Cloud, and registers your machine. On a headless machine, the sign-in prints a URL and a one-time code that you can approve from your phone.

Open [app.pontia.dev](https://app.pontia.dev), select your machine, activate a project directory as a Workspace, and start a session with the pi Agent Client. The [Dashboard guide](/docs/dashboard) covers the details.

## Use the TUI on the same session

Sessions started from the Dashboard run in tmux sessions whose names start with `pontia_`. To open one in your terminal:

```sh
tmux ls
tmux attach -t SESSION_NAME
```

Detach with the usual tmux key binding. The session keeps running and stays available in the Dashboard.

## Use a custom pi command

If you run pi through a wrapper script or from a non-standard path, set the command in `$PONTIA_HOME/config.toml`:

```toml
[runtime.pi]
tui_command = "/absolute/path/to/pi"
```

Then run `pontia up` to apply it.

## Multi-stage tasks

For larger tasks, Pontia [Workflows](/docs/workflows) run a sequence of pi sessions with explicit handoff files between stages. Each stage gets a fresh context, and you can follow every stage's session from the Dashboard.

## Troubleshooting

Start with `pontia doctor`. If a pi session does not report activity, check the hook log:

```text
$PONTIA_HOME/state/pi-hook.log
```

Also confirm that the plugin is still installed and that `pontia status` reports a healthy service. See [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) for more.

